<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1433–<a href="/years/1504">1504</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Stephen III of Moldavia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen the Great was Prince of Moldavia from 1457 to 1504, the principality's outstanding ruler. He defeated an Ottoman army at Vaslui in 1475 and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> to defend Moldavian independence, though late in his reign he accepted paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. He founded many churches and monasteries, including Putna, and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-of-mangup">Maria of Mangup</a> came from Theodoro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great.jpg" alt="Stephen the Great" width="320" height="753" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninhursag3 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%9Etefan_cel_Mare_(Stephen_the_Great)_1488,_Vorone%C5%A3_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stephen was the son of Bogdan, himself a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-good">Alexander the Good</a>, Prince of Moldavia, and of Maria Oltea. His birth date is unknown, and historians estimate it between 1433 and 1440. The death of Alexander the Good in 1432 began a succession crisis of more than two decades. Bogdan seized the throne in 1449 with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>, and Stephen was styled voivode in his charters as his heir and co-ruler. In 1450 father and son repulsed a Polish invasion. Peter III Aaron murdered Bogdan in October 1451, and Stephen fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He won the throne in 1457 at the Battle of Orbic with the support of his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlad-the-impaler">Vlad the Impaler</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Stephen captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a> in January 1465 and ordered the Church of the Assumption at Putna in 1466 to commemorate it. After repulsing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baia-romania">Baia</a>, he stopped paying tribute to the Ottoman sultan in 1473. He then invaded Wallachia to replace Radu the Fair, an Ottoman-installed vassal, with his own protégé, Basarab III Laiotă, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>. Each prince he put on the throne was soon forced to pay homage to the sultan. In 1475 he defeated a large Ottoman army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vaslui">Battle of Vaslui</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> called him Athleta Christi, Champion of Christ, though promised support never came.</p><p>The following year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> routed Stephen at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valea-alba">Battle of Valea Albă</a> but withdrew from Moldavia for lack of provisions, a failed siege of Neamț and an outbreak of plague. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and their Crimean Tatar allies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Cetatea Albă</a> in 1484, and Stephen's efforts to recover them failed. From 1486 he again paid a yearly tribute to the Ottomans. He played <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the Ottomans against each other, and defeated the Polish army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert">John I Albert</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-cosmin-forest">Battle of the Cosmin Forest</a> in 1497. In 1502 he and his Crimean Tatar allies destroyed the remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> at the Battle of Samara River. He acknowledged the loss of his ports to the Ottomans in 1503.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stephen died on 2 July 1504 after a reign of more than 47 years, during his last years sharing government with his son Bogdan III. He restored old fortresses and built new ones, and from 1487 more than a dozen stone churches were built at his initiative, among them Voroneț in 1488 and Tazlău in 1496 to 1497. They mixed Byzantine and Gothic elements with local tradition and helped form a Moldavian style of architecture. The Romanian Orthodox Church canonised him in 1992, and he is regarded as a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stephen the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Stephen the Great</b> (r. 1457–1504) made the most of these assets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stephen the Great</b>, prince of Moldavia, defeated the Hungarian king Matthias Corvinus, who was wounded three times, at Baia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-15">15 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Theodoro's princes married into the ruling families of Trebizond and Moldavia; Maria of Mangup became the wife of <b>Stephen the Great</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-theodoro">Principality of Theodoro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Borzești, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.24,26.82&amp;year=1479" aria-label="Borzești on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5183 536 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stephen the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5633 806h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5633 806h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.24,26.82&amp;year=1479">What was here in 1479? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
